<PageTitle>Culture</PageTitle>

<section>
    <h1>Culture</h1>

    <p>
        The culture of the Bahamas is a vibrant blend of West African heritage, British colonial history, and American influences, shaped by island life across the archipelago.
    </p>
</section>

<section>
    <H2>Junkanoo</H2>
    <p>
        Junkanoo: The country's most famous cultural expression—an energetic, indigenous street parade held on Boxing Day (December 26) and New Year's Day (January 1). It features dazzling handmade cardboard and crepe-paper costumes, goatskin drums, cowbells, and whistles.
    </p>

    <H2>Music</H2>
    <p>
        Rake ’n’ Scrape: A traditional folk music style born on Cat Island, centered around an accordion, goatskin drum, and a carpenter's handsaw scraped with a metal file
    </p>


    <h2> Food & Cuisine </h2>
    <ul>
        <li>
            Conch (pronounced konk): The national staple, served fresh as citrus-marinated conch salad, deep-fried conch fritters, or cracked conch.
        </li>
        <li>
            Peas and Rice: Made with pigeon peas, rice, salted pork, and local spices, frequently paired with stewed fish or baked crab.
        </li>
        <li>
            Guava Duff & Johnnycakes: Sweet island comfort foods, with guava duff being a rolled dessert pastry served with warm butter-rum sauce.
        </li>
    </ul>
</section>